WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Okay, with all the opportunities. So,

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.120
Ari Kas, Crushing, Somewhere, obviously

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.319
Crushing, Bolt Storage is. So, those are

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.000
your big your big three. There's other

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:09.599
ones you have involved as well, but I

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.599
want to make sure we we mention the book

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:13.519
and how this fit in because there's so

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:14.639
much that you have going on. When did

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:15.280
you decide to write a book in the first

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.520
place, Nick?

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.760
>> So, I sold my book to Harper Collins and

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.800
got my advance two weeks before Marshall

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.800
called me about this somewhere deal.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.760
So, I I committed to writing I committed

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.640
to writing a book. I committed to uh you

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.239
know telling all my stories and putting

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.920
my entire business philosophy when it

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:39.280
comes to sales management delegation

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.879
opportunity in order and in a like the

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.879
book I wish I would have read before I

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.440
started my first business. Basically I

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:46.800
committed to doing that project a couple

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.780
weeks before the somewhere opportunity

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.559
fell in my lap. That's just it's such an

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.120
[laughter]

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:54.800
it's it's just such a it has so many

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.559
parallels to every area of life. It's

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.719
like you're never ready. The timing is

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:00.000
never right. It's timing is never right

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:01.359
to have kids. The timing is never right

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.400
to start a business. There's always a

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:06.320
bunch of reasons not to do something. Um

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.080
I had just signed my book deal and that

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.600
was a huge reason not to do the deal

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.159
with Marshall because it was going to

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.240
take a ton of my time. Um but writing a

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:16.000
book was it's been an amazing journey,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.400
man. Like the hardest part of writing.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.560
So most business books you're going to

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.240
get you're going to be lucky if you walk

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.799
away with three or four good ideas that

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.799
can that you can put in your in your in

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:28.880
your business. I wanted to get three or

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:30.479
four good ideas across to the reader per

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.079
chapter.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.159
I wanted to tell a ton of stories. Like

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:35.439
I've I've had I've had a lot of

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:37.360
experiences in the trenches of running

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.320
these companies. A lot of stress, a lot

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.479
of things that worked, a lot of things

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.320
that didn't. But I've also talked to

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.320
thousands of other entrepreneurs cuz I

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.799
my network has exploded over the last,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.079
you know, five years. So, one of the

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:52.000
hardest parts of writing the book was

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:55.119
telling all their stories and either

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.799
saying their name or finding a way to

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:58.399
tell the story so that they weren't

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:59.759
identifiable because they didn't want to

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:01.920
be identifiable. [laughter]

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:03.119
But yeah, man, it's like there's some

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.960
basics of running a company and

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:08.479
operating and hiring and selling and

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.679
managing people and, you know, tell the

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.360
stories to back it up. So, I think it's

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:13.520
going to be a great book.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.480
>> Why that time in terms of like deciding

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.599
then that you want to write a book? is

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.400
that you could could have waited five

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:17.680
more years, could have wait 10 more

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.200
years, you could have done it earlier.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.120
Like why that timing?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.360
>> I I had been thinking about it for 3

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.239
years. Um I wanted to do it back in

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.400
2021. I talked to a couple publishers.

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:29.520
Um first of all, publishers like yes,

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.879
they want you to write a good book and

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.239
the win for them is a book that sells

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:34.000
for a long time, but when they give a

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:37.200
book deal, they only care if you can

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.360
sell a book. So luckily, I had 200,000

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.599
people on my email newsletter. I had my

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.080
Twitter following. I had my podcast. I I

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.920
can sell books. So, I got I had the book

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.400
deal on the table, but they told me it

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.319
was going to take 18 months.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.480
And I'm like, that doesn't make any

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:54.800
sense. I can write really fast. Like, I

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.599
can write 60,000 words in a weekend.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.760
Like, this is this is a no-go for me.

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:01.040
Like, I'm used to writing a piece of

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.200
content, putting it on Twitter, and

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.120
getting feedback 3 minutes later or 30

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.800
seconds later. I don't want to write

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.560
something and get feedback 18 months

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.760
later. So, I put it off. I put it off. I

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.680
put it off. And finally, I realized

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.200
like, Nick, you have to do this. like

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.959
you you're going to start forgetting

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.239
these stories. Um, and your future self

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.159
will be really glad that you wrote a

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:23.120
book.

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:24.400
So, that's that's why I did it.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.840
>> How long did it take to actually write

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.680
the thing?

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.519
>> Well, I thought it was going to be easy.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.360
I So, I put all my tweets, all my most

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:32.560
popular tweets, my 200 most popular

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.159
tweets, they all had over a thousand

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.519
likes, put them on a spreadsheet, and

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:37.840
hired a ghostriter. I was like, "Hey,

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:39.360
write the book."

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.799
He wrote a chapter. He was amazing. He

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.280
wrote a chapter and I just realized,

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:44.400
hey, the the easy button is not going to

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.080
work here, Nick. Like, this is your

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.840
legacy. This is your book. You have to

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.120
write this. So, I had a conversation

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.640
with him like, man, I'm going to write

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.879
this book. I'm really sorry. I led you

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.760
on here. Paid him paid him what I owed

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:57.360
him and moved on. Um, then I then I

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.680
spent 3 months writing the book and it

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.040
and it was 90,000 words instead of 60,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.959
which my editor, you know, they wanted

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.560
to most business books do great if

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:07.599
they're really digestible and really to

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.879
the point, 220 words, 240 words. I wrote

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:12.879
320 words and the editor is like,

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:15.840
first of all, Nick, like it's too long.

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:17.359
Second of all, this book sucks. Like,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.959
you can't write a book like you like you

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:21.359
tweet. This doesn't make any sense. It's

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:25.360
not cohesive. It doesn't it doesn't tell

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:26.400
a story. Um, rewrite this. And by the

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.160
way, you got to go hire this other

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.520
editor who knows the structure and how

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.680
to like coach you along on writing a

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:37.120
book. So, it's July

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.960
of 2024 and the book is due in September

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.160
and I rewrote the book in July and

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.199
August. So, it was a it was a pain in

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.479
the ass, man. It was tough. The There's

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:48.320
highs where you write a chapter and

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:49.680
you're like, "Holy if I could have

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.520
read something like this when I was an

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.199
early entrepreneur, this would have

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.639
changed everything for me." And then

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:58.639
there's times where I

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:00.080
am just like, who am I to write this

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:03.919
book? nobody's gonna want to read this

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.000
book. And I just had anxiety around like

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:08.560
didn't have permission, right? So it it

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.240
was a it working on something for 18

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.400
months with no feedback is really really

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.400
tough.

